// Signing for the SipAI target.
//
// A fresh clone signs to run locally — ad hoc, no Apple account, no
// edits — so `git clone` then ⌘R just works. That is what the empty
// SIPAI_TEAM below is for. A team id hardcoded here would fail on every
// machine that is not a member of it, which is the failure this file
// exists to remove.
//
// To sign with your own team WITHOUT touching the project file, drop a
// `Local.xcconfig` beside this one holding one line:
//
//     SIPAI_TEAM = ABCDE12345
//
// It is gitignored, and it is included before the settings below, so
// that one line is enough — the identity follows from it. Selecting a
// team in Xcode's Signing & Capabilities pane works too, but that writes
// the team into project.pbxproj, which is exactly what breaks the
// clone-and-build promise for everyone else.
//
// Worth doing if you run the app rather than just build it: macOS
// identifies an ad-hoc build by the exact bytes of its binary, so every
// rebuild looks like a different app and asks again for access to
// Desktop, Documents and Downloads. A real team identity gives the build
// a stable designated requirement, and the grant sticks.
//
// Release builds read none of this — `Release/release.sh` passes the
// Developer ID identity and team on the xcodebuild command line, where
// they override both this file and the project.

SIPAI_TEAM =

#include? "Local.xcconfig"

DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = $(SIPAI_TEAM)
CODE_SIGN_STYLE = Automatic

// The identity has to follow the team, or setting only SIPAI_TEAM
// leaves this at "-" and hands back an ad-hoc build that looks signed.
// With no team, `$(SIPAI_TEAM:identifier)` is empty, so this resolves to
// SIPAI_IDENTITY_ below — ad hoc. With a team it resolves to an
// undefined variable, i.e. empty, and an empty identity under automatic
// signing is what makes Xcode pick the right certificate itself.
SIPAI_IDENTITY_ = -
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = $(SIPAI_IDENTITY_$(SIPAI_TEAM:identifier))
